A||||4||BAM-BAM||3.4L...Which heads do I use?||||5||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||05-08-2000||08:22 AM||||   With the 3.4L upgrade, which heads am I supposed to use? I believe, in an earlier post, that it was mentioned that the 2.8 heads were reused. Is that true? And if so, what type of valve work should be done? just a couple of prelem questions. <P> Thanks, BAM-BAM  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||05-08-2000||09:45 AM||||The Fiero V6 heads are considered high output because of the larger valves and will fit.||||reg||1
Z||000002||N_Man||05-08-2000||01:09 PM||||Don't the Camaro heads have even larger exaust valves and fit as well?||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||05-08-2000||01:21 PM||||This is a standard list of what needs to be done at the machine shop to any used heads.<P>1) Hot tanked (cleaned)<BR>2) Magnafluxed (check for cracks)<BR>3) Inspection (check guides, seats, surface straightness, etc.)<BR>4a) On high performance rebuild, buy new valves, springs, and retainers<BR>4b) On stock rebuild, valves and springs must be perfect (shim springs to specs)<BR>5) Replace or repair any bad guides and rocker studs (use screw in studs for hipo)<BR>6) Have the valves and seats treated to a multi-angle valve grind.<BR>7) Mill (or plane) the head surface if needed<BR>8) If you still have extra money and this is a hipo motor, pocket porting helps flow too||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||05-08-2000||06:48 PM||||The Camaro/Firebird 3.4 has the same heads as the 2.8 Fiero, so it would just be easier to use the heads that came with the 3.4.  That is unless you find a good deal on a short block 3.4, then you could just install the Fiero heads and save a few $$.<P>||||reg||3
